=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r8
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** OSPF virtual link VLINK0 — the sole mechanism connecting OSPF area 0.0.0.2 to the backbone (area 0.0.0.0).

**Mechanism:** r8 serves dual roles: it is an ABR for area 0.0.0.2 (hosting loopbacks 18.1.1.1–18.1.4.1/24 and segment 80.0.0.0/8 on eth1) and the far endpoint of an OSPF virtual link that traverses transit area 0.0.0.1. The near endpoint is r4 (`area 1 virtual-link 18.1.4.1`) and r8 reciprocates (`area 1 virtual-link 14.1.4.1`). When r8 was restarted, its ospfd process terminated, tearing down both the physical adjacency with r5 (eth0/58.1.1.2) and the virtual-link adjacency with r4. With VLINK0 down, r4 withdrew its type-3 inter-area LSAs for area 0.0.0.2, causing prefixes 18.1.x.x/24 and 80.0.0.0/8 to disappear from every backbone router's OSPF LSDB and FIB — explaining "part of the network gone missing from the screens."

**Localization:** r8, OSPF process, VLINK0 virtual interface and its physical backing on eth0 (58.0.0.0/8, transit area 0.0.0.1 towards r5/r4).

**Current state:** r8's ospfd reconverged approximately 18 minutes ago. VLINK0 is Full (neighbor 14.1.4.1/r4, Up 17m43s), inter-area routes for area 0.0.0.2 are installed in every router's FIB, and end-to-end pings from pc0 to 18.1.4.1 and 80.1.1.1 succeed with 0% loss. The link has self-recovered.

**Root cause:** r8 was restarted, collapsing the OSPF virtual link and temporarily isolating area 0.0.0.2 from the backbone.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required now — OSPF has fully reconverged. To prevent a repeat outage during future planned restarts, configure OSPF Graceful Restart on r8 (`router ospf` → `graceful-restart`) so that its adjacencies — including VLINK0 — are maintained or quickly re-established without withdrawing LSAs.
=== END ===